With the unique advantages of excellent visible-light response, facile synthesis, non-toxicity, high stability, etc., graphitic carbon nitride \(\left(\mathrm{g}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\right)\) has been considered as a very promising photocatalyst for catalyzing water splitting, organic degradation and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction [33–47]. However, the photocatalytic efficiency of pure \(\mathrm{g}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) is still limited because of the fast recombination of photogenerated electrons and holes. Considering that \(\mathrm{g}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) contains a high level of homogeneous nitrogen atoms, which can offer abundant binding sites for stabilizing highly active metal centers, researchers have focused on the deposition of transition metals (e.g., Fe, Co, Ni, Mn, etc.) on the surface of \(\mathrm{g}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) to promote the charge separation and boost photocatalytic activity [33, 38–40]. Although great efforts have been devoted, the facile and general approach to load transition metals on \(\mathrm{g}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) to prepare atomically dispersed metal photocatalysts needs to be further developed.
Herein, we report a one-step calcination method to prepare atomically dispersed metal photocatalysts based on \(\mathrm{g}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\). Specifically, by pyrolysis of a Co-MOF during the process of \(\mathrm{g}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) formation using urea as a precursor, the confined Co atoms in the MOF could be transferred and anchored on the \(\mathrm{g}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) ultrathin layer, affording a series of Co atomically dispersed X-Co-C\(_{3}\)N\(_{4}\) (X represents the amount of Co-MOF used) photocatalysts with different Co contents (Scheme 1). The optimal 25-Co-C\(_{3}\)N\(_{4}\) catalyst exhibits high catalytic performance for the photochemical reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{CO}\), with
the CO generation rate reaches \(394.4\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\), over 80 times higher than that of pure \(\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}\) \((4.9\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1})\). In addition, using a Fe-MOF or Mn-MOF instead of the Co-MOF, the Fe/Mn atomically dispersed \(\mathrm{Fe-C_{3}N_{4}}\) and \(\mathrm{Mn-C_{3}N_{4}}\) photocatalysts have been also obtained under the same conditions, manifesting the reliability and universality of this strategy in synthesizing photocatalysts with highly dispersed metal centers.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials and instruments
All chemicals were purchased from commercial sources and used without further purification. Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) was collected on a smart X-ray diffractometer (SmartLab \(9\;\mathrm{kW}\), Rigaku, Japan) with \(\mathrm{Cu}\;\mathrm{K}\alpha\) radiation \((\lambda=1.54178\mathrm{~\AA~})\). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was carried out on Netzsch STA 449C Thermogravimetry Analyzer. Photoluminescence (PL) spectra were measured with an F-4600 fluorescence spectrometer. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were acquired on a transmission electron microscope with a LaB6 Gun (Tecnai G2 Spirit TWIN, FEI, USA) at an acceleration voltage of \(120\;\mathrm{kV}\). High-angle annular dark field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) measurements were taken on a transmission electron microscope with a probe corrector (Titan Themis Cubed G2 60-300, FEI). Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping images were taken on a high-resolution transmission electron microscope with FEG (Talos F200X, FEI, USA). X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) were conducted by using an X-ray spectrometer (ESCALAB 250 Xi spectrometer, Thermo Scientific, USA) with Al \(\mathrm{K}\alpha\) as the excitation source. Gas sorption measurements were conducted using a multi-station specific surface micropore and vapor adsorption analyzer (BELSORP-Mas, MicrotracBEL, Japan). Solid-state UV–Vis absorption spectrum was obtained on a UV–Vis spectrophotometer (UV-3600, Shimadzu, Japan). Mott-Schottky plots and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) were performed on an electrochemical workstation (CHI660E, China). The Co content of each photocatalyst was quantified by an inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometer (ICP-MS, iCAP RQ, Germany). Catalytic products were analyzed by a gas chromatograph (GC-2014+ATF, 230C, Shimadzu, Japan) equipped with two automated gas sampling valves, which contain a thermal conductivity detector (TCD) and a flame ionization detector (FID). The detection for the \({}^{13}\mathrm{C}\) isotope in \({}^{13}\mathrm{CO}\) was analyzed using mass spectrometry (HPR-20 QIC). Time-resolved fluorescence (TRF) spectra were tested by using fluorescence spectrophotometer (Horiba FL-3, HORIBA Scientific, USA).
## 2.2 Synthesis of Co-MOF
Co-MOF was synthesized according to the literature method [48]. Briefly, a mixture of \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((279\;\mathrm{mg})\), imidazole-4, 5-dicarboxylic acid \(\mathrm{(H_{3}D C,~156~m g)}\), pyrazine (pyz, \(80~\mathrm{mg}\)), NaOH \(\left(60\;\mathrm{mg}\right)\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \(\mathrm{[0\mL]}\) was heated at \(180\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(72\,\mathrm{{h}}\) in a Teflon-lined stainless steel reactor. After the autoclave
cooled to room temperature, wine-red crystals of Co-MOF were collected by filtration and washed with distilled water. Yield: \(68\%\).
## 2.3 Synthesis of Fe-MOF and Mn-MOF
The Fe-MOF and Mn-MOF were synthesized following the same procedure as that of Co-MOF except for the addition of \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}\) \(\left(192\;\mathrm{mg}\right)\) and \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((381~\mathrm{mg})\) instead of \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((279\;\mathrm{mg})\).
## 2.4 Synthesis of \(\mathbf{X-C o-C_{3}N_{4}}\)
The mixture of urea \(_{(5.0\,\mathrm{g})}\) and Co-MOF (10, 15, 20, 25, 30 or \(35~\mathrm{mg}\) ) was heated at \(550\;^{\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{C}\) for \(4\,\mathrm{{h}}\) in the air. The dark yellow powder was collected and washed by deionized water and ethanol, which was dried under vacuum at \(60~^{\circ}\!C\) for \(24~\mathrm{{h}}\) to yield \(\mathrm{X-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) \(\mathbf{\Delta}(\mathbf{X}=10,\,20,\,25,\,30\) or 35, represents the amount of Co-MOF used).
## 2.5 Synthesis of \(\mathbf{Fe-C_{3}N_{4}}\) and \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n-C}_{3}\mathbf{N}_{4}\)
The \(\mathrm{Fe-C_{3}N_{4}}\) and \(\mathrm{Mn-C_{3}N_{4}}\) were prepared from Fe-MOF/ Mn-MOF \((25\;\mathrm{mg})\) and urea \(_{(5.0\,\mathrm{g})}\) following the same procedure as that of \(25\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Co-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\).
## 2.6 Synthesis of \(\mathbf{g-C_{3}N_{4}}\)
The pure \(\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}\) was synthesized by a same procedure as that of \(25\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Co-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) in the absence of Co-MOF.
## 2.7 Photocatalytic \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) reduction
A mixture of photocatalyst \((2.0\;\mathrm{mg})\), \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((4.0\;\mathrm{mL},\;\nu/\nu=\) 3:1), triethanolamine (TEOA, \(1.0\;\mathrm{mL}\)), and \(2,2^{\prime}\)-bipyridine \((15.0\;\mathrm{mg})\) was added into a \(17.5\;\mathrm{mL}\) quartz test tube. The reaction system was degassed with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to remove \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and other gases, followed by irradiation upon a \(300\;\mathrm{W}\) Xe lamp with a \(420\;\mathrm{nm}\) filter. The collected gaseous products were detected using GC.
## 2.8 Photoelectrochemical measurements
15, 20, 25, 30 and \(35~\mathrm{mg}\) Co-MOF and \(5.0~\mathrm{g}\) urea in the air, respectively. All \(\mathrm{X-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) catalysts show similar XRD patterns, with two peaks at \(13.0^{\circ}\) and \(27.5^{\circ}\), which correspond to the typical (100) and (002) crystal planes of \(\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}\), respectively. No characteristic diffraction peak of Co nanoparticles is observed, suggesting that the Co atoms are highly dispersed on \(\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}\) layers (Fig. S5 in the ESM). The contents of Co in \(\mathrm{X-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) catalysts, determined by inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS), increase from \(0.99\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) to \(5.87\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) along with the increased amounts of Co-MOF used (Table S1 in the ESM). TEM, HAADF-STEM and EDS were further conducted to thoroughly reveal the morphology and composition of \(\mathrm{X-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\). As shown in Fig. 1(a), the TEM image of \(25\mathrm{-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) shows that the morphology of \(\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}\) is ultrathin layers and no Co nanoparticles are observed, which is in accordance with the powder XRD result. The HAADF-STEM image exhibits that a large number of isolated Co sites anchoring on \(\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}\) are clearly observed (Fig. 1(b)). EDS mapping further shows the homogeneous distribution of C, N and Co (Fig. 1(c)). These results imply that a \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\)-supported photocatalysts with atomatically Co atoms highly dispersed have been successfully prepared.
To further examine the chemical composition and elemental states of \(25\mathrm{-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\), XPS measurements were carried out. The result demonstrates that C, N, O, and Co elements are included in \(25\mathrm{-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) (Fig. 2(a)). The two main characteristic peaks located at 796.1 and \(781.3~\mathrm{eV}\) for Co 2p are assigned to the Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) and Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) of \(\mathrm{Co^{II}}\), where the binding energy at \(781.3\;\mathrm{eV}\) can be attributed to the Co-N coordination (Fig. 2(b)). Moreover, the shakeup peaks of Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) and Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) centered at 803.6 and \(786.2\;\mathrm{eV}\) respectively are characteristic of a high-spin
\(\mathrm{Co^{II}}\) species [49]. The N 1s spectrum shows four characteristic peaks centered at the binding energies of 398.9, 399.7, 401.0 and \(404.6\;\mathrm{eV}\), which are assigned to pyridinic N, Co-N, graphitic N and oxide N, respectively (Fig. 2(c)) [29]. The two characteristic peaks at 288.4 and \(284.9\;\mathrm{eV}\) can be found in the C 1s spectrum corresponding to \(\mathrm{N-C=N}\) and C–C, respectively (Fig. 2(d)) [36]. The above results suggest that the highly dispersed Co centers are anchored on the surface of \(\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}\) ultrathin layer.
To explore the universality of this facile synthetic strategy, we attempted to synthesize other atomically dispersed single-metal-site photocatalysts along this approach by varying MOF precursors. A Fe-MOF and a Mn-MOF were prepared according to the literature method (Fig. S6 in the ESM) [48]. SEM images of Fe-MOF and Mn-MOF also show rod-shaped morphology (Figs. S4(b) and S4(c) in the ESM). Both MOFs were respectively used as precursors instead of Co-MOF to prepare \(\mathrm{Fe/Mn-C_{3}N_{4}}\) by the same one-step calcination strategy. The powder XRD patterns of the resulted \(\mathrm{Fe/Mn-C_{3}N_{4}}\) exhibit characteristic peaks assignable to \(\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}\), and no characteristic diffraction peak corresponding to \(\mathrm{Fe/Mn}\) nanoparticles was observed (Fig. S7 in the ESM), which is similar to that of \(\mathrm{Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\). TEM images also show ultrathin layer of \(\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}\) and no generation of Fe and Mn nanoparticles (Fig. S8 in the ESM). These results verify that \(\mathrm{Fe-C_{3}N_{4}}\) and \(\mathrm{Mn-C_{3}N_{4}}\) with highly dispersed Fe and Mn sites were successfully obtained, and thus solidly evidence the reliability and universality of this synthetic strategy.
The porous feature of \(\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}\) and \(25\mathrm{-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) has been investigated by \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption at \(77\;\mathrm{K}\). The results suggest that the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of \(25\mathrm{-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) is \(90.2\;\mathrm{m}^{2}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which is slightly larger than that of \(\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}\) (\(53.4\;\mathrm{m}^{2}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), Fig. S9 in the ESM). The pore size distribution of \(25\mathrm{-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) is in \(2{-}6\;\mathrm{nm}\), indicating abundant mesopores within \(25\mathrm{-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\), which are beneficial to the mass transfer during the catalytic process (Fig. S10 in the ESM). Solid-state UV–Vis spectrum of \(25\mathrm{-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) demonstrates strong light-harvesting ability in the UV and visible regions, which is similar to \(\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}\) (Fig. S11 in the ESM). Based on this solid-state UV–Vis spectrum, band gap energy \((E_{\mathrm{g}})\) of \(2.83\;\mathrm{eV}\) was calculated for \(25\mathrm{-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) by the Kubelka-Munk (KM) method (Fig. S12 in the ESM). Moreover, to elucidate its semiconductor character, Mott-Schottky measurement of \(25\mathrm{-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) was conducted at the frequencies of 500, 1,000 and \(1,500\;\mathrm{Hz}\). The positive slope of the \(C^{-2}\) values (vs. applied potentials) reveals the character of an n-type semiconductor. The flat band position, determined from the intersection point, is \(-0.93\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) (\(-0.73\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. SHE), which is approximating to the bottom of the conduction
band (LUMO) (Fig. S13 in the ESM). In addition, the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms and solid-state UV–Vis spectra of 10-Co\(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\), 15-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\), 20-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\), 30-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) and 35-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) present similar results to those of 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) (Figs. S9 and S11 in the ESM). Given the more negative LUMO position of 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) compared with the potential needed for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to CO \((-0.51\,\mathrm{V}\) vs. NHE), it is theoretically feasible for 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) to photoreduce \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to CO [50].
On the basis of the above results, the photocatalytic reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{X-Co-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) has been investigated with TEOA as the sacrificial agent and \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CNH}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) as solvent \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) can afford a good solubility for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and stabilize the photoinduced charge separation, leading to the efficient electron transfer to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) under visible light irradiation [50]. As shown in Fig. 3(a), 10-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) exhibits a small CO production rate of \(32.8\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\). With the increased amount of Co-MOF used for catalyst preparation, the CO production rates remarkably enhanced. When the amount of Co-MOF used increases to \(25~\mathrm{mg}\), the CO production rate of the resulted 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) photocatalyst reaches \(394.4\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\). Further increase of the Co-MOF amount to 30 and \(35~\mathrm{mg}\), the CO production rate drop to 262.3 and \(227.5\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) respectively, which might be attributed to the aggregation of the Co active sites. Therefore, the photocatalytic activity of \(\mathrm{X-Co-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) exhibits a volcano-type trend, and the optimal 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) shows almost 12 and 1.7 times higher than that of 10-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) and \(35\mathrm{-Co-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\), respectively. Moreover, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production rate is only \(94.5\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) for 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\), unveiling that most photogenerated electrons were used for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction with high selectivity of \(81\%\) (Fig. 3(b)). For comparison, the pure \(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) was prepared by pyrolysis of urea in the absence of Co-MOF. The result of photocatalytic experiment shows that the \(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\), featuring ultrathin layers, displays much lower photocatalytic activity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction, with the CO production rate of only \(4.9\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) (Figs. S14 and S15 in the ESM). In addition, the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction experiments by the physical mixture of \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) \((\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\&\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})\), as well as the physical mixture of \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{IDC}\), pyz and \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) \((\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\&\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{IDC}\&\mathrm{pyz}\&\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})\) were carried out. The results show that the CO production rates for the former and latter are 124.5 and \(137.5\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\), respectively. These values manifest the superiority of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\)-based photocatalyst with highly dispersed Co centers (Fig. S15 in the ESM).
Additionally, a series of control experiments of photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction in the absence of photocatalyst, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), or light
irradiation were carried out. The results demonstrate that no products can be detected, indicating that all these elements are indispensable for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to CO. To further verify the origin of CO, the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction experiment was performed over 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) by using \({}^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) instead of \({}^{12}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). As shown in Fig. 3(c), a signal peak at \(m/z=\) 29 corresponding to \({}^{13}\mathrm{CO}\) was detected in the mass spectrum, affirming that the carbon source of CO indeed comes from \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) [51]. Moreover, the durability of 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) was examined by recycling photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction experiments. No noticeable decrease of catalytic activity is observed after three runs (Fig. 3(d)). This result, coupled with the retained powder XRD and atomically isolated Co atoms after catalytic reaction, demonstrating that 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) possesses good durability during the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction process (Figs. S16 and S17 in the ESM).
In order to elucidate the higher activity of 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) than pure \(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption, photocurrent, EIS and PL spectra measurements for 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) and pure \(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) were carried out [52–54]. The 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) displays much higher \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake than that of pure \(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) at 1 atm and \(298\,\mathrm{~K~}\), which might be responsible for the significantly improved activity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photoreduction (Fig. 4(a)). The results of photocurrent measurements show that 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) possesses a higher photocurrent response than pure \(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\), demonstrating the better charge separation by 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) (Fig. 4(b)). The EIS of 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) shows a smaller radius than pure \(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\), hinting the lower charge-transfer resistance in the former (Fig. 4(c)). Moreover, the time-resolved PL spectrum of 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) presents a reduced average exciton lifetime (1.73 ns) compared with that of pure \(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) (3.51 ns), indicating the enhanced charge transfer, which can be attributed to the band-tails-involved electron-hole recombination (Fig. 4(d)) [55, 56]. In addition, the results of PL spectra show 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) presents a pronounced emission quenching than \(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\), further implying the suppressed electron-hole recombination in 25-Co- \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) (Fig. S18 in the ESM). These results unambiguously suggest that the deposition of atomically dispersed Co sites on \(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) by one-step calcination method facilitates charge separation, and thus boosts the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction activity.
a series of \(\mathrm{Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) photocatalysts featuring highly dispersed Co sites anchoring on \(\mathsf{g}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) have been fabricated. The photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction experiments demonstrated that \(25\mathrm{-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) exhibits the highest activity for the reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to CO, with the generation rate of \(394.4~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\), which is over 80 times higher than that of pure \(\mathsf{g}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\). The incremental photocatalytic performance of \(25\mathrm{-Co-C_{3}N_{4}}\) over pure \(\mathsf{g}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) could be attributed to the improved \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake and charge separation efficiency. This work provides a new way for development of cost efficiency MOF-derived catalysts for photochemical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction.
